0","name":"Exploit Public-Facing Application","parent":null,"tactics":["Initial Access"],"behavior":"User wardog executed a Python POC script (public-poc.py) targeting the local Exchange server with credentials wardog@azsentinel.local, exploiting a known Exchange vulnerability.","slot":0,"reasoning":"The evidence shows execution of a public POC script targeting a local Exchange server, which is the direct exploitation of a known vulnerability in an Internet-facing application. While the script uses credentials, the primary adversary objective here is exploiting the Exchange vulnerability itself, not collecting email or establishing persistence\u2014those would be post-exploitation objectives. T1190 is the correct initial access technique for this exploit attempt.","evidence":[{"event_id":"E016","field":"CommandLine","quote":"python public-poc.py localhost wardog@azsentinel.local","ok":true},{"event_id":"E016","field":"Image","quote":"C:\\Users\\wardog.MXS01\\AppData\\Local\\Programs\\Python\\Python39\\python.exe","ok":true}],"attempts":1},{"technique_id":"T1003.001","name":"LSASS Memory","parent":"OS Credential Dumping","tactics":["Credential Access"],"behavior":"IIS worker process (w3wp.exe) executed commands and accessed the LSASS named pipe, indicating credential dumping or authentication token theft through a deployed webshell.","slot":1,"reasoning":"The evidence shows w3wp.exe connecting to the LSASS named pipe, which is the direct mechanism for accessing LSASS process memory for credential dumping. While the webshell delivery is notable, the operator's objective here is credential access via LSASS memory, not persistence\u2014the pipe connection is the credential dumping technique itself, not merely a side effect of command execution.","evidence":[{"event_id":"E021","field":"PipeName","quote":"\\lsass","ok":true},{"event_id":"E021","field":"Image","quote":"c:\\windows\\system32\\inetsrv\\w3wp.exe","ok":true},{"event_id":"E041","field":"ParentImage","quote":"C:\\Windows\\System32\\inetsrv\\w3wp.exe","ok":true}],"attempts":1},{"technique_id":"T1505.004","name":"IIS Components","parent":"Server Software Component","tactics":["Persistence"],"behavior":"A malicious ASPX file (ohyeah.aspx) was created in the Exchange OWA authentication directory and compiled by the ASP.NET runtime, providing persistent web-based command execution.","slot":2,"reasoning":"The operator created a malicious ASPX file in the Exchange OWA authentication directory and the ASP.NET runtime compiled it, establishing persistent web-based command execution through IIS components. This is a direct match for T1505.004 (IIS Components) rather than T1056.003 (credential harvesting), as the objective is persistence via a malicious web component, not credential capture.","evidence":[{"event_id":"E026","field":"TargetFilename","quote":"C:\\Program Files\\Microsoft\\Exchange Server\\V15\\FrontEnd\\HttpProxy\\owa\\auth\\ohyeah.aspx","ok":true},{"event_id":"E040","field":"TargetFilename","quote":"C:\\Windows\\Microsoft.NET\\Framework64\\v4.0.30319\\Temporary ASP.NET Files\\owa\\8e05b027\\e164d61b\\ohyeah.aspx.f5dba9b9.compiled","ok":true},{"event_id":"E034","field":"ParentImage","quote":"C:\\Windows\\System32\\inetsrv\\w3wp.exe","ok":true}],"attempts":1}],"unverified":[],"declined":[],"hypotheses":[{"behavior":"User wardog executed a Python POC script (public-poc.py) targeting the local Exchange server with credentials wardog@azsentinel.local, exploiting a known Exchange vulnerability.","event_ids":["E016","E017"],"keywords":["ProxyLogon","ProxyShell","Exchange RCE","public POC","exploitation"]},{"behavior":"IIS worker process (w3wp.exe) executed commands and accessed the LSASS named pipe, indicating credential dumping or authentication token theft through a deployed webshell.","event_ids":["E021","E041","E044"],"keywords":["webshell","LSASS access","credential dumping","named pipe","w3wp.exe"]},{"behavior":"A malicious ASPX file (ohyeah.aspx) was created in the Exchange OWA authentication directory and compiled by the ASP.NET runtime, providing persistent web-based command execution.","event_ids":["E026","E040","E034"],"keywords":["webshell","ASPX","OWA","persistence","ASP.NET compilation"]}],"retried":[]},"trace":[{"t":1.74,"agent":"hunter","kind":"text","text":"I'll analyze these events systematically to identify the adversary behaviors."},{"t":1.75,"agent":"hunter","kind":"tool_result","tool":"search_events","result":{"matches":2,"events":"<log_events trust=\"untrusted\">\n[E016] +38s MXS01 Sysmon 1 process created: User=MXS01\\wardog IntegrityLevel=High ParentImage=C:\\Windows\\System32\\cmd.exe Image=C:\\Users\\wardog.MXS01\\AppData\\Local\\Programs\\Python\\Python39\\python.exe CommandLine=\"python public-poc.py localhost wardog@azsentinel.local\"\n[E017] +38s MXS01 Security 4688 process created: SubjectUserName=wardog ParentProcessName=C:\\Windows\\System32\\cmd.exe NewProcessName=C:\\Users\\wardog.MXS01\\AppData\\Local\\Programs\\Python\\Python39\\python.exe CommandLine=\"python public-poc.py localhost wardog@azsentinel.local\"\n</log_events>"}},{"t":10.05,"agent":"hunter","kind":"text","text":"Now I have a clear picture. Let me analyze the key behaviors:\n\n1. **E016-E017**: User \"wardog\" runs a Python script \"public-poc.py\" targeting localhost with wardog@azsentinel.local credentials - this is an exploitation attempt (likely ProxyLogon/ProxyShell based on the Exchange context).\n\n2. **E026, E040**: An ASPX webshell \"ohyeah.aspx\" is created in the Exchange OWA auth directory, compiled and executed by w3wp.exe (IIS).\n\n3. **E021**: w3wp.exe connects to the lsass named pipe - this indicates credential dumping or LSASS access from the web application.\n\n4. **E041-E045**: w3wp.exe executes cmd.exe and whoami - command execution through the web application.\n\n5. **E034-E036**: w3wp.exe spawns cvtres.exe (resource compiler) - this is part of the ASP.NET compilation process triggered by the malicious ASPX file.\n\